ion  is how much spare capacity  would be enough um given that  uncertainty  so we built further on these models and  on these data  we took the high dense area again same  area  we introduced 12 24 and 48 percent spare  in three scenarios  and then we said okay let's then assume  we built that network with that spare  that is reserved for 5g  and let's assume in five years from now  we  we know at that time what the 5g looks  like  we consider it to be the high cell  density scenario  with 95 coverage on 26 gigahertz  and let's see how much of that future  network fits into our  spare capacity so it's a bit  similar but uh with a level of  uncertainty at the time we built the  fiber network  again we're using microducts in the  assumptions we're  building it based on a certain  number of ducts or subducts that we  actually reserve for 5g  averaging out to these 12 24 and 48  and then illustrating it here you see  that  we would actually deploy these micro  duct bundles  for fiber to the home but reserving  always a minimum  spare ducts the blue ones there that  should not be used for anything else in  the future 5g network  and then we see depending on how much  power there is  that the 5g might fit into it for some  parts  and then we avoid double trenching but  for some parts the capacity is  full and there may still be  additional trenching because we didn't  anticipate enough  capacity looking at the costs  you can see here a standalone at 8.5  million  euro as before the standalone 5g at 8  1.8 million but we also discount that  over five years so the net present value  with eight percent would be 1.25 so we  have a total net present value of  building two separate  non-converged networks and then if we  introduce twelve percent spare  we see a very slightly increase of the  cost  not a lot because we already have ducts  that we can  reserve for this and we see a decrease  of the 5g  so reusing that spare capacity is good  but still overall the decrease is not  significant  going to 24 we see the increase a bit  further  but especially there we see a big  decrease of the 5g  cost um going to 48 that increase  a bit higher but the decrease is not  spectacular anymore  and to reference at the previous result  uh of building it today knowing what 5g  would look like  i would actually end up with an almost 9  million  euro bringing that together um the  conclusion here in this case stacking  those different values you clearly see  that well 12 in these cases with these  assumptions was  clearly not enough we didn't get the  full value of the convergence out of  this network  um but in these assumptions with the 24  we were able to capture the majority  of that value of convergence  or put a bit differently here you see  the um  the the how this  cost for 5 key can be reduced  by reusing the different levels of spare  capacity  now i do have to warn a bit with with  this result  that um what we clearly do not want to  state is that  every case 24 will be the ideal or  best spare capacity for 5g  um again it depends on the assumptions  the case the  the density and the parameters that you  would put in  um but nevertheless again a very  realistic result  based on realistic inputs from start to  finish  um and interesting to see that you do  need to foresee the right level of spare  capacity  um because 5g is quite heavy on fiber  demand in the future  um so um yeah 12 is already significant  but clearly not enough  under these assumptions  okay so our key findings of the whole  study  was that there was a huge cost saving  for building the fiber to the home and  the 5g network together  this was the first result between 65 and  96  of the 5g fiber cost could be  eliminated by building it together  at least for the underground scenario um  then um we also looked at if you would  build it today  fiber to the home with only limited  spare capacity 12  or lower well you would incur additional  costs for the future 5g network  over a reasonable lifetime of the fiber  it's not like the fiber network is going  to be  renewed in that time frame so we ended  up with two to three and a half times as  expensive to roll out fiber for 5g  this is also one of the reasons why we  definitely believe that areas where  there is  spare capacity in fiber rollouts today  will be  the areas where 5g networks could be  leading in the future  because of course this will impact the  business case enormously  if that fiber spare capacity is  available or not  um and then lastly as a let's say a  tip uh good practice uh is  okay often you don't know yet what that  future 5g  really densified scenario will look like  but there is a way to anticipate on it  and that's by building sufficient spare  capacity  into the networks of today  so um that's uh  let's say a walk through our complete 5g  convergence  model and study i would definitely like  to thank  our contributing members and people from  the working group  on this study uh who contributed a lot  with the  discussions on assumptions model and  calculations and results  and i'm happy to hand over to tom to  treat any questions that we may have  thank you ralph and uh we'll let you  catch your breath there for a second  because i  think that was a whistle-stop tour of a  a couple of very large studies and i  think  some of these uh these slides are  available already  on the fth council website from previous  seminars and the fta th conferences  and there's also a white paper that  we've published on on this topic also  that can be downloaded  from the ftth council website so if  anybody has  interest in exploring the topic further  i would uh certainly encourage them to  go and  look at those um initially  um maybe good to mention tom just  quickly is that  um it's indeed um this presentation  combines a lot of slides from  previous workshops and study uh  presentations so i do think indeed the  good thing is this is a bit of a total  overview of everything we did  over the past three years so definitely  uh  the best version if you're interested a  lot of the other presentations  on the website will typically only  highlight a part of this so this is a  i think a nice uh overall um  overview of everything yep although i  think there's  very few people who could uh go through  it all as quickly as you have in the  last  35 minutes and take it all in um  i have a just a couple of questions ref  um  does uh i guess one on the um  the the first study regarding the the  savings  um of building a converged network  versus building  um standalone networks if you look at  um say the uh  either the high cell density or the  medium cell density scenario  and you consider going from a densely  populated area to a suburban area to a  medium area or to a low populated area  you can see that the you know the the  savings seem to  increase and then they decrease again  is there any you know reason why  why why that occurs is or is it maybe  within the just the  the boundaries of error of the  simulations  um yes i think so um  it's indeed it's a very good observation  um  i don't have a complete logical  explanation for it  except that indeed uh what we really do  is take  yeah three areas three um different  cases and we calculated and these are  the numbers  um and and results in this kind of  simulations  is not always completely covered by the  assumptions you're looking at there's  indeed sometimes  other assumptions underneath that could  influence for example one  one influencing factor could be the  difference  in the unit cost of trenching  between a suburban and an urban area  but we we have not been able to really  pinpoint or  or find it out um so i think the  the the conclusion of this is that  every case is different and it's it's  what we want to avoid a bit is that  these numbers would be used also  as um the truth and the  the barriers and the and the extrapolate  on this where you are  no actually every area can have  different aspects  we illustrate in material areas but  it's not yeah 100 guaranteed that the  same number will apply to every medium  dense area for example  that's fair okay um and then  i'm relating to the uh the second study  which i think  you know probably is it uh  of great interest of people who are  considering right now building ftth  networks and trying to understand  what their spare capacity policy should  be because that  truly has to be one of the big questions  is what's going to be the need in the  future how many fibers do i  really need how many dots do i really  need etc and this  this has put some interesting numbers on  the table there  um just one thought that struck uh one  question we received  regarding this was the sparing policy  here seems to be around  spare duct um and if you had  taken an approach of instead of maybe  using  96 fiber cables to go with a 288 fiber  cable or a 432 fiber cable  would that deliver  similar savings or would it greatly  increase the day one cost of the ftth  network  so much that it it doesn't make make  sense so  um that's a you know the comparison  between  duct sparing versus fibrous bearing as  your day once bearing policy  yeah yeah yeah it's a very good question  you could actually  uh perfectly also do it on a  on a fiber level to fossil spare fibers  for future 5g  in fact well i briefly touched upon that  aerial case  where on ariel yeah you would probably  have to do it that way to foresee  immediately the fibers if you really  want to  benefit from the convergence but the  reason why we  did take the duct level is that you do  have a lot of flexibility indeed  building the duct network on day one is  not the most expensive  and indeed in the end five years later  in this  scenario when we then uh know where 5g  comes  obviously we only blow the necessary  fiber capacity  through the empty ducts where they're  needed  which also indeed means we're not  putting a lot of cables in the ground  where they're not needed  and so it is indeed a bit of a balance  between the pre-investment  and then keeping a flexibility to  actually deploy additional capacity  where  ultimately you need it in five years  from now that you may not  know today so it's indeed a scenario  well we could calculate it on a fiber  level as well but it would indeed  incur the additional cost and then yeah  i'm not sure  overall if that will be a more cost  efficient way  um for the underground scenario against  okay thank you um we have a  question coming on on the webinar as  well uh to what extent do you consider  5g millimeter wave as a compliment or a  substitute to ftth  i guess that's as much a question for  the council as it is  for the people presenting this webinar i  would say that the uh  the council's uh position on that would  be that the the  technologies are complementary um and  that there will always be a need for  for you know the the fixed network  termination point  to uh to deliver stuff that we don't  even even know about yet so it's uh it's  definitely a complimentary technology  and my  my from my perspective and be happy for  you to maybe chip in there as well if  you have anything  anything else to say on that yeah i i  obviously fully agree  in the sense also that if you looked at  some of the numbers i  shared if you want to really deliver a  high  bandwidth to to all residential users  you have two problems i think with uh  with 5g  only for that and assuming you wouldn't  need the fiber to home  the two things is one you would need to  go into the high frequencies because  we're talking about  bandwidth needs that are uh very high  especially for home users  more and more that bandwidth need is  high so you're talking about  moving very closely with your antenna so  fixed wireless access examples on 60  gigahertz  for example in us have been deployed but  then you  also have that issue of the higher the  frequency the more the line of sight  and the even lik